                <pb facs="0852" n="756" sameAs="frus:frus1926v02/pg756"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">881.822/112: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Diplomatic Agent and
                        Consul General at Tangier</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Blake</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-02-08T18:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">February 8,
                                1927—6 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>1. Your despatch 155, January 20. The Department perceives no objection to the
                    adoption by the International Commission of the procedure suggested in Mr.
                    Fayard’s memorandum, provided that due regard is paid to the reservations
                    formulated in your letter of June 3, 1926, to the President of the
                    Commission.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
